Cell[CellGroupData[{

Cell["", "SlideShowNavigationBar", "FirstSlide", CellTags->"SlideShowHeader", CellOpen->False],

Cell["Just-In-Time Machine Learning (JITML)", "Title"],

Cell["A self-evolving architecture", "Subtitle"],

Cell["Ghetea Patrick", "Text"],

Cell["Copyright (c) 2026 Ghetea Patrick. All rights reserved.", "Caption"]

}, Open]],

Cell[CellGroupData[{

Cell["", "SlideShowNavigationBar", CellTags->"SlideShowHeader", CellOpen->False],

Cell[CellGroupData[{

Cell["Abstract", "Section"],

Cell["Just-In-Time Machine Learning (JITML) is a proposed way to adapt a model while it is running. Instead of relying on one fixed task network for every situation, a suite of meta-networks generates compact specialists when an unfamiliar context appears.", "Text"],

Cell["This notebook presents the architecture and a small red-and-blue tile experiment. The Python snippets are the supplied structural template: their formatting and placeholders are preserved, and they are displayed as non-evaluating code blocks.", "Text"]

}, Open]]

}, Open]],

Cell[CellGroupData[{

Cell["", "SlideShowNavigationBar", CellTags->"SlideShowHeader", CellOpen->False],

Cell[CellGroupData[{

Cell["The limits of a fixed starting point", "Section"],

Cell["Ahead-Of-Time Machine Learning (AOTML), as used here, means preparing a network before deployment and keeping its task structure fixed during use. That starting point works while the situations it encounters remain close to what it was prepared for. A change in context can call for an update or a different specialist.", "Text"],

Cell["JITML explores a different runtime path: keep a useful baseline, reuse specialists for familiar contexts, and generate a new task network when the existing ones no longer fit. The aim is to handle a new context without retraining the entire system at every encounter. The meta-networks still need an initial training process and later updates.", "Text"]

}, Open]]

}, Open]],

Cell[CellGroupData[{

Cell["", "SlideShowNavigationBar", CellTags->"SlideShowHeader", CellOpen->False],

Cell[CellGroupData[{

Cell["Core components", "Section"],

Cell["Task-networks (TNNs) do the immediate work. Each is a compact feed-forward neural network associated with a field of expertise: a group of contexts for which it is useful.", "Text"],

Cell["Meta-networks (MNNs) build those specialists. The suite separates four decisions: SNN defines the shape, ANN chooses activations, WNN supplies weights, and BNN supplies bias values. The proposed MNNs also use feed-forward designs.", "Text"],

Cell["Two pipelines connect them. The Task Generation Pipeline (TGP) creates a specialist at runtime. The Meta Training Pipeline (MTP) learns from accumulated context–TNN pairs so that later specialists can start from better proposals.", "Text"]

}, Open]]

}, Open]],

Cell[CellGroupData[{

Cell["", "SlideShowNavigationBar", CellTags->"SlideShowHeader", CellOpen->False],

Cell[CellGroupData[{

Cell["Task Generation Pipeline (TGP)", "Section"],

Cell["An unfamiliar context enters a sequence of generators. Each stage fills in one part of the new network definition, building on the choices made before it.", "Text"],

Cell["SNN · Shape", "Subsubsection"],

Cell["The SNN proposes which neuron slots are active. In the proposed design, sigmoid outputs are interpreted as an on/off mask; slots that do not contain neurons are removed.", "Text"],

Cell["ANN · Activations", "Subsubsection"],

Cell["The ANN uses the structural description to choose the activation function for each layer.", "Text"],

Cell["WNN and BNN · Parameters", "Subsubsection"],

Cell["The WNN fills the network with weights, and weights close to zero can be pruned. The BNN completes the bias values, matching the gen_biasvalue() interface in the Python template. The intended result is a compact TNN before candidate selection begins.", "Text"],

